# Cursor app seats

Cursor Desktop/Agents Window is the normal runtime. The main checkout and
ordinary branches are a **readiness bridge**: reads and `.pytest-verify-tmp/`
scratch are free. Governed shell mutations and remote Git effects surface one
in-app approval; native file-tool edits outside a bound Director deny because
Cursor does not currently enforce `preToolUse` approval.

A live seat is one fact: a linked worktree on reserved branch
`cursor-seat/<seat>` whose user-local registry record (root, active top-level
app `conversation_id`, app-visible selected model ID) was written by the
`sessionStart` hook. Wrappers and hooks resolve that identity at point of use;
environment variables describe it but never grant authority. Cursor app seats
use their worktree's native Git index and reject `GIT_INDEX_FILE`.

- Standing pair: `director` plus `operator`; `director2` / `operator2` /
  `coordinator` are cold capacity. Model diversity is required only when the
  executable risk profile requires it.
- `director`/`director2` implement without per-action approval in their own
  worktree.
- `operator`/`operator2` review and issue GO/NITS/FAIL; their one unattended
  commit is the exact event path just staged by the fixed writer.
- Bound Director/Operator `cursor-publish` / `cursor-consume` do not need a
  second mailbox approval. Do not ask chat to re-authorize the same effect
  after a hook approval card.
- `coordinator` reconciles on demand, authors no production changes, and holds
  no mailbox cursor.
- Behavior-changing acceptance requires a non-author Operator against one
  committed verify-request. High-risk control additionally requires a
  different model family and abuse-class assessment; the selected model ID is
  app-visible evidence, not provider/backend attestation.

Never edit mailbox, lock, or `.cursor/runtime/` state directly — the hook
hard-denies that. Push, pull, fetch, merge, rebase, and paid spend are separate
remote effects; each needs its own in-app approval for the exact action.

Never switch branches inside a seat chat on a dirty tree; the app may
auto-commit a checkpoint. Seats stay on their reserved branch.

Custom subagents are optional advisors or capacity workers, launchable from
any chat including bound seats (`.cursor/agents/` defines the read-only
advisors). They are not durable seats, cannot inherit seat authority, and
cannot publish verdicts. Full seats are pinned top-level Agents Window chats
in dedicated worktrees.

App capabilities — subagents, skills, in-app browser (including the optional
ChatGPT Pro consultation skill), plan mode, and MCP — are available at seat
discretion. Every well-formed MCP invocation asks through the fail-closed hook
because that payload has no documented child discriminator. The card is
invocation consent, not external-effect authority. A capability never adds
task, review, or effect authority.

Use side chats only in readiness or unreserved workspaces. In a reserved seat
worktree a new side-chat conversation may supersede the pinned session binding;
use a custom read-only subagent there instead.
